<?php
/**
 * Operator notices — WHO SEES THEM  (v1.0.0-beta.11)
 *
 * Copy this file to operator.php to change who sees operator-facing notices in
 * the admin panel (today: "a QuickSite update is available"). QuickSite writes
 * operator.php for you at first run, naming the account that created the
 * install, so on a normal installation there is nothing to do here.
 *
 * ── IT GRANTS NOTHING ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * This list decides whether a NOTICE RENDERS. That is all it does. Naming an
 * account here gives it no command, no permission and no access it did not
 * already have, and no code may use this list to authorize an action.
 *
 * That is deliberate and it is the whole design. QuickSite has no
 * installation-wide role: a permission is either "any signed-in account" or
 * "nobody", and everything else is per-project. A file that could name
 * "the administrator of this server" would put that principal back, and with it
 * the escalation it brings. A display preference cannot.
 *
 * The reason it exists at all: notices like "update available" are addressed to
 * whoever maintains the SERVER. Someone invited to edit one project's text is
 * not that person — they cannot act on it, and it tells them which engine
 * version the install is running.
 *
 * ── ⚠ IF YOU ARE ABOUT TO MAKE THIS LIST DO MORE ─────────────────────────────
 *
 * IT MAY ONLY EVER GATE DISPLAY. NEVER CAPABILITY.
 *
 * The moment an ACTION is gated on it — a command, a button that does something,
 * a permission check — this stops being a display preference and becomes the
 * installation-wide role QuickSite deliberately does not have. Not something
 * like it. The same thing, under a new name.
 *
 * It will not look like that when it happens. It arrives as a convenience, and
 * the reasoning sounds right every time: "the operator is the only one who could
 * act on this anyway", "we already load the list here", "it only reads". Each of
 * those is a true sentence and the wrong move — because once an
 * installation-wide principal exists, there is a path to becoming it, and
 * QuickSite's whole permission model is built on there being no such principal.
 *
 * If an action genuinely needs an operator, it does not belong on the web
 * surface at all: it belongs in a script run from a shell, where the credential
 * is filesystem access. That is why updating QuickSite is update.sh and not an
 * API call. A thing only an operator may do is a thing a shell does.
 *
 * ── WHO THE OPERATOR IS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Whoever has filesystem access to the server. That is not a role QuickSite
 * hands out; it is a fact about the machine. Somebody who can edit this file
 * can already edit users.php, so they hold strictly more than any list could
 * grant — which is exactly why editing a file, and not calling an API, is how
 * this changes. The first-run setup token works on the same principle.
 *
 * ── FORMAT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * User ids, as they appear in secure/management/config/users.php — the
 * 'usr_…' array keys, NOT usernames. Any number of them, including none.
 *
 * DEFAULT WHEN THIS FILE IS ABSENT: nobody. A missing, unreadable or malformed
 * file reads as an empty list, never as "everybody" — so a notice meant for the
 * server's maintainer is never shown to an account by accident.
 */

return [
    'operators' => [
        // 'usr_0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef',
    ],
];
